1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,490
A payroll software provider serves many business customers.

2
00:00:04,490 --> 00:00:13,000
Each wants to ask an AI assistant about its
own data, without exposing anyone else's.

3
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:17,480
Here, a bakery and a car garage use separate Claude sessions.

4
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:25,000
Both connect through the same MCP access-control service.

5
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:28,930
The bakery asks about its payroll.

6
00:00:28,930 --> 00:00:36,770
In two separate real test sessions, Claude
retrieves an October cost of €11,857,

7
00:00:36,770 --> 00:00:47,000
and an increase of €213 over September.

8
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:53,300
But what happens if the bakery tries to access a garage employee's payslip?

9
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:57,480
Claude makes the request, but the MCP server blocks it.

10
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:10,000
No private data is revealed, and the attempt is recorded in the audit log.

11
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:16,390
Meanwhile, the garage uses its own Claude
session to retrieve its five employees.

12
00:01:16,390 --> 00:01:24,000
It only receives the records it's authorized to see.

13
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:31,600
A scripted test client also verifies the local OAuth
and PKCE flow before Claude receives its test token.

14
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:37,000
Automated tests check that access restrictions are enforced.

15
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:42,830
One shared integration. Separate customer
data. Access control enforced by the server.

16
00:01:42,830 --> 00:01:51,000
This is a local engineering demonstration using
fictional payroll records, not a production deployment.
